ers. This preserves potential melds in the T suit (T2-T3-T4) and avoids giving opponents a chance to complete sequences.\nReason: Discarding T9 minimizes the risk of aiding opponents' melds while maintaining the possibility of forming additional chows or pairs in the T and B suits. It also aligns with the terminal-heavy structure of the hand, which could contribute to scoring patterns like \"All Terminals\" if needed later.\nAnswer: Play T9"}
