ep the B1s in hand. \n\nTherefore, the best action is to pass.\n</think>\n\nEvaluation: The player has a concealed kong of B5 and a chow of T5-T6-T7. Penging B1 would add a Pung of B1 (terminal tiles), but the remaining tiles (W7, W8, T2, T3, B8) cannot form valid melds to complete the hand. The total score would likely fall below the 8-point minimum. Passing preserves flexibility for future draws.\nReason: Penging B1 adds minimal points (1 for Pung of terminals) and does not ensure a winning hand. The concealed kong of B5 (4 points) and potential future draws offer a better path to reaching the required score.\nAnswer: Pass"}
